00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.189 align:start position:0%
 
what we're going to do now is we're  going to talk about student loans  there's a massive problem with student  loans in this country so if you think  about the student loan problem here in  america we've got a ton of different  numbers but first we're going to show  you this big chart of just how bad it is  there's a historic total national  student loan debt balance this is  measured in trillions of dollars  remember the number you're about to see  is measured in trillions not billions  not millions not thousands it's measured  in trillions of dollars and what we have  seen is since 2003 it has just been up  and to the right can't stop won't stop  that's all this chart does is it goes  higher and higher and higher if there  was a way to bet on a financial asset  and that financial asset was is the  student loan debt in america going to go  up or down you could go yolo long  levered and you'd still make money  because it only goes up we now are over  1.7 trillion dollars in the entire  national student loan debt balance now  when we actually break it down by the  numbers what we see is that there's 43.2  million student borrowers that are in  debt and they have an average of 39 000  of student loan debt each  each of the 43 million students have  39 000 of student debt on average the  average public university student  borrows thirty thousand dollars to  attain a bachelor's degree  in may of 2020 nine percent of borrowers  who attended public institutions were  behind on their student loan payments so  not only do we get a situation where  there's tens of millions of people who  have student loans but they have very  big balances over thirty thousand  dollars is the average balance meaning  fifty percent of it is higher than that  on top of that these student loans many  times have pretty egregious interest  rates that you have to pay given what  they are especially that we're giving  them to young people and then you add in  that in may of 2020 nine percent of  people were behind i'm sure that that  number has actually increased over time  given the financial hardship that many  people have experienced and so when we  look at this problem you can see that  it's really really bad now the private  student loan debt is not necessarily  much better the private loan borrowing  cons constitutes eight percent of the  outstanding student loan debt and the  national private student loan balance is  137  billion dollars so there's a market for  the public student loans right then  there's the private market where people  actually go and borrow student loans as  well so 137 billion dollars on top of  what is being done publicly now the  sources of this we've got a chart that  shows the educational debt among  borrowers student loans 95  credit 23  other 11 home equity loans are only 4  now when you look at this chart and you  see the sources of educational debt  among borrowers it is so damn high now  when we actually start to break this  down 5.8 percent is the average student  loan interest rate among all student  loans both federal and private the  average federal loan interest rate is  4.1  so when you start to actually look at  this four to five percent interest rates  on 30 plus thousand dollar balances for  tens of millions of americans it is  really really really bad now this is the  average interest rates for all federal  loans you can see that it was all the  way up at seven percent it came down to  six it kind of oscillated between five  to six percent and then recently in 2020  and into 2021 it dropped all the way  down to 4.2 percent or so the 4.2  percent is really important because what  the 4.2 percent says is that compared to  seven you pay drastically less but that  doesn't necessarily mean that the  problem is solved when you have this big  of a student loan problem billions and  billions and billions in the private  market 137 billion or so but then also  the federal student loans is over 1.7  trillion dollars  tens of millions of people holding it  and paying four to five percent today  they have been paying six or seven  percent over the last few years there  are people who graduated college 10  years ago 15 years ago and they still  haven't paid off their student loans  that's a problem when we have young 18  to 22 year olds who are going into the  workforce and they are already starting  behind the starting line they're not  starting out just like everyone else  they're starting out actually in the  negative they have debt and they've got  to work their asses off to get out of  debt one of the things that you can do  is you can actually go and you can get a  refinancing of your student loan so this  is one of the biggest tips that we're  ever going to tell you on this show now  i am not an expert in refinancing  student loans but right now after i  spent a couple of hours talking to the  team over at sofi they walked me through  exactly how this works so if you have  let's say a federal student loan that  federal student loan that you may be  paying four or five percent on and that  is on average tens of thousands of  dollars for tens of millions of people  what you can do is you can go and so far  has done this now for over 375 000  people which is pretty nuts number and  you can actually go and get a lower rate  by refinancing it's just the same way  that you would have a mortgage and then  if the interest rates drop you can  actually refinance your mortgage you can  do the exact same thing with your  student loans and so when you think  about this idea of these private student  loans as an option to refinance it  becomes a really powerful tool  in your financial journey

